WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.750
INTRO:
Warren Buffett is generally regarded as the

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:08.670
best investor of all time which is not surprising
given his insane track history.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:14.860
He has managed to achieve an average annual
return of 20.5% since 1965.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.220
And while Buffett does deserve all of the
praise he receives, someone that often doesn’t

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.610
get enough credit is Charlie Munger.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:27.492
Charlie is the vice chairman of Berkshire
Hathaway and has known Buffett since 1959.

00:00:27.492 --> 00:00:31.100
Buffett says that he knew that he wasn’t
going find another guy like Munger as soon

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.140
as he met him.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.149
So, hiring Munger was a no brainer for Buffett.

00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:39.840
Since then, Munger has Buffett’s closest
ally and business partner, and together, they’ve

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.430
made Berkshire’s most iconic investments
such as Coca Cola.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.570
Today, Charlie is a multi billionaire himself
thanks to the phenomenal performance of Berkshire

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.570
Hathaway.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.920
Yet, Munger is still heavily underrated amongst
the general public especially in comparison

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.829
to Buffett, so here’s how Munger become
the back bone of Berkshire Hathaway.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:03.679
EARLY LIFE:
Taking a look back, Charles Thomas Munger

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:07.550
was born on January 1, 1924 in Omaha, Nebraska.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.290
Munger had a pretty nice childhood as he came
from a pretty well off family.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:16.150
Munger’s grandfather himself was a district
court judge and a state representative.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.990
Meanwhile, Munger’s father was a lawyer.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.570
Considering this background, I don’t think
you’d be surprised to hear that Munger would

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.520
eventually end up pursuing a law degree.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.800
Anyway, as a teenager, Munger would find a
modest job a local grocery store named Buffett

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:28.800
&amp; Son.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.750
As you would guess, this store was owned by
Buffett’s grandfather, and this was Munger’s

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.570
first introduction to the family.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.440
Munger would go onto attend the University
of Michigan where he majored in mathematics.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.580
But, just as he enrolled, Pearl Harbor would
be bombed resulting in Munger dropping out

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.190
of college and enlisting in the US Army Air
Corps which would eventually become the Air

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.190
Force.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.800
While this was quite a turn in events, it
wasn’t all bad for Munger.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.410
He would soon be stationed in Alaska where
he met a woman named Nancy Huggins who would

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.780
soon become his wife.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.730
Apart from this, however, Munger found little
joy in the Air Force.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.670
He was numbed by all the death and destruction
that he saw on a daily basis.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.750
One of the few things that kept him sane during
this time period was playing cards with his

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.069
colleagues, and this is where he learned many
of the principles that he would later apply

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:12.390
to investing.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.090
Munger says that cards taught him to fold
early when the odds are against him and double

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.870
down when he had the edge.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.150
Playing cards also taught him that opportunity
doesn’t come often which makes it especially

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.790
important to seize an opportunity when it
does come.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.499
Anyway, the war would finally come to an end
in 1945, and Munger would leave the military

00:02:29.499 --> 00:02:30.860
shortly after.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.790
By this point, Munger didn’t want to go
back and finish his bachelors, but he did

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:36.439
want to pursue a law degree.

00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:40.379
Fortunately, the GI Bill would help Munger
do exactly this.

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:44.310
He went ahead and took a bunch of advanced
courses at Caltech, and he would leverage

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.970
this experience to sneak his way into Harvard
Law school without a bachelors.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.650
Despite this, law school was a breeze for
Munger as he would end up graduating magna

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:58.150
cum laude in 1948, and with that, Munger was
officially a lawyer.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:03.459
EXTREME ADVERSITY:
After graduation, Munger and his new family

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:07.640
would move to California where he found a
job at a law firm called Wright &amp; Garrett.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.060
For most people, this would be the pinnacle
of life as Munger had secured a solid job

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.489
and family, but it wouldn’t take long for
everything to unravel.

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:20.069
After 8 years of marriage and 3 children,
Munger and Nancy would decide to split ways

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:23.730
which was not only challenging emotionally
but also financially.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.760
Munger would end up losing most of his money
and his house to the divoce.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.219
And, Munger would end up spending the next
few years simply working back to where he

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:31.219
was.

00:03:31.219 --> 00:03:35.040
To make things worse, divorce was heavily
stigmatized at the time which didn’t earn

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.079
Munger much respect from his peers.

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:40.920
And just when you think things couldn’t
get worse, they got way worse.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.480
Munger’s son, Teddy, would be diagnosed
with leukemia.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.040
Munger didn’t have health insurance, so
he not only had to pay for all the treatment

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.779
out of pocket, but he had to slowly watch
his son get weaker and weaker for 11 months

00:03:52.779 --> 00:03:55.370
before passing away at age 9.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.340
Around the same time, Munger’s father would
pass away which forced Munger to return home

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.430
and support his family for some time.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.359
The one good thing that came out of this visit
is that he would end up meeting a man named

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:06.599
Warren Buffett.

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:10.799
The two would quickly become friends and exchange
contact info, but both of them would return

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:13.629
to their own lives after this visit was over.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:17.019
After returning to California, Munger would
eventually develop cataracts which forced

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:19.440
him to get surgery in his left eye.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.010
Unfortunately, this surgery would go wrong
which left him blind in his left eye.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.970
He held onto the blind eye for some time,
but he eventually got it surgically removed

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.720
due to severe pain.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.449
To make things worse, doctors told him that
blood would eventualy fill up his right eye

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:36.139
as well which would make him completely blind.

00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:40.720
So, Munger would actually begin learning braille,
but thankfully, Munger would finally catch

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.060
a break and the condition would slowly recede.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.460
Clearly, Munger had a quite a difficult life
throughout his 30s and 40s, but here’s what

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.780
Munger has to say about it.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.500
“You do not want to drift into self-pity…

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.470
Self-pity will not improve the situation.”

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:59.930
GIVING UP LAW:
Before returning to Nebraska for his father’s

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:02.560
death, Munger would actually find a second
wife.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.190
Ironically, her name was also Nancy, but this
marriage would end up working far better than

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:10.000
the first despite it being unorthodox for
the time.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.560
Both Munger and Nancy had two children each
from their previous marriages, and together,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.090
they would form a new family of 6.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.360
These two would end up staying together up
for the next 54 years until Nancy passed away

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.360
in 2010.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.610
Anyway, after his father’s death, Munger
decided start fresh by launching his own business

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:27.319
in 1962.

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:31.659
This new business was called Munger, Tolles
&amp; Olson and it provided real estate attorney

00:05:31.659 --> 00:05:32.659
services.

00:05:32.659 --> 00:05:36.180
While his day job was still being a lawyer,
Munger spent most of his freetime meddling

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.690
with the stock market.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:42.240
He had his ups and downs and the income wasn’t
super consistent, but Munger saw opportunity

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.879
in the stock market and he didn’t waste
much time seizing it.

00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:48.821
Soon enough, he left law altogether and partnered
with Jack Wheeler to launch an investment

00:05:48.821 --> 00:05:50.690
firm called Wheeler, Munger, and Company.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:54.229
At first, Munger saw a significant amount
of success.

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:58.400
For years, he was averaging 20% annual returns
which was 4 times higher than the market at

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.400
the time.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.319
But, the 1970s would bring with it a lot of
pain, not just for Munger, but all investors

00:06:04.319 --> 00:06:05.319
in general.

00:06:05.319 --> 00:06:09.909
In 1971, President Nixon would drop the gold
standard which sent inflation to the moon

00:06:09.909 --> 00:06:11.570
and stocks to rock bottom.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:16.289
The S&amp;P 500 would face multiple bear markets
throughout this decade, and stocks would basically

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:17.870
make no progress.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:22.910
Munger was by no means immune to this market
downturn, and his investments would get crushed.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:28.669
In 1973, Munger would lose 32% and in 1974,
Munger would lose another 31%.

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:33.479
Fortunately, the stock market had a massive
rebound in 1975 which allowed Munger to post

00:06:33.479 --> 00:06:35.880
a 73.2% return.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.689
Munger took this as a signal that he should
leave while he still could and he would close

00:06:39.689 --> 00:06:42.039
up Wheeler, Munger, and Company in 1976.

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:44.970
And that marks the end of Munger’s days
of running his own business.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.009
A few years later in 1978, Munger would join
Buffett’s company as vice-chairman and the

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:51.979
rest is history.

00:06:51.979 --> 00:06:57.220
BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY:
While Munger technically always worked under

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.969
Buffett on paper, Buffett never treated him
as an employee.

00:07:00.969 --> 00:07:03.960
Rather, Buffett treated Munger as a business
partner.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:09.040
In 1984, Munger was made as the CEO and Chairman
of Wesco Financial which was a company that

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.651
Berkshire Hathaway had a majority stake in.

00:07:10.651 --> 00:07:14.860
And this is where Munger would spend most
of his time at Berkshire.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:19.150
Wesco Financial was primarily an insurance
company, but Munger was allowed to operate

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.719
Wesco financial as a sort of mini Berkshire
Hathaway.

00:07:21.719 --> 00:07:25.819
Through Wesco, Munger had the opportunity
to invest in companies like Coca-Cola, Kraft

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:28.699
Foods, Procter &amp; Gamble, and Wells Fargo.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:32.770
This was perfect for Munger because Wesco
was headquartered in Pasadena, California

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:35.430
which had been Munger’s home for most of
his adult life.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:40.110
He served as the CEO and chairman of Wesco
up until 2011 when Buffett would purchase

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:43.979
the remaining portion of Wesco and integrate
it completely into Berkshire.

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:48.150
Aside from his role at Wesco, Munger was also
the chairman of the Daily Journal which was

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.479
yet another Berkshire Hathaway business.

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:54.270
As the name suggests, the Daily Journal was
a publishing company, but news publishing

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.419
was never Munger’s top priority.

00:07:56.419 --> 00:08:00.800
Rather, Munger primarily focused on acquiring
other publishing companies and investing the

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.970
company’s profits especially in China.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:07.210
Munger is actually a massive fan of China
and Alibaba was one of the Daily Journal’s

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.530
biggest investments.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:13.569
As you would guess, Munger is not a big fan
of the conflict between the US and China whatsoever.

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:16.629
He says that “They should like us and we
should like them.”

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:20.860
Nonetheless, due to the strain between the
US and China over the past few years, Munger

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:24.199
has reluctantly reduced his Alibaba position
significantly.

00:08:24.199 --> 00:08:27.539
This is one of the last things he did before
stepping down from the Daily Journal a few

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:28.539
months ago.

00:08:28.539 --> 00:08:32.750
Moving onto Munger’s final major responsibility,
we have his role at Costco.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.750
Munger become a director at Costco in 1997
around the time that Berkshire invested into

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:37.930
the company.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:41.810
Berkshire exited their position in Costco
in 2020, but Munger still personally owns

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.260
$81 million worth of Costco stock.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:47.880
Munger is a massive fan of Costco’s business
model and he thinks that the company has great

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.000
culture and ethics.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:53.380
He doesn’t necessarily think that Costco
is a value buy at today’s prices, but he

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.510
also doesn’t see a reason to sell out.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.380
And that brings us to where we are today.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.490
MUNGER’S LEGACY:
Like Buffett, Munger has turned much of his

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.060
focus to philanthropic activities.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.010
One of his biggest contributions has been
to universities.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:12.290
For example, he was a massive donor to the
University of Michigan.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:15.480
He has also donated hundreds of millions to
UC Santa Barbara.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.550
Something that we should note, however, is
that Munger has not signed the Giving Pledge,

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:22.640
and he has actually been super transparent
about this.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.040
He says that he’s already given too much
of his money to his children to take any credit

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.540
for joining the giving pledge.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.670
This has sparked some criticism against Munger,
but honestly, it’s his money, and he should

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.850
be able to do whatever he wants with it.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.160
Something else that has sparked a lot controversy
is Munger’s position on Bitcoin.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.019
He has claimed that Bitcoin is “disgusting
and contrary to the interests of civilization.”

00:09:43.019 --> 00:09:45.889
He has also called Bitcoin “rat posion”.

00:09:45.889 --> 00:09:49.650
His biggest critiques against the crypto are
that it has no fundamental value and that

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:51.440
it produces no cash flow.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.620
Munger and Buffett aren’t big fans of gold
either, so it’s not surprising that they

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.100
don’t buy into Bitcoin.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:01.149
Another sector in which Munger has missed
out on is growth stocks and especially tech

00:10:01.149 --> 00:10:02.149
stocks.

00:10:02.149 --> 00:10:06.240
They never really invested into any of the
FAANG companies except for Apple, and even

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.681
there, they were late to the party.

00:10:08.681 --> 00:10:12.580
But, the cool thing is that Munger and Buffett
have managed to still roughly match the S&amp;P

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.930
500 despite missing out on tech companies.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.389
It looks like they’ve chosen to stick with
what they know, and this strategy has played

00:10:18.389 --> 00:10:19.470
out extremely well for them.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:23.390
Also, I’m not sure how much either of them
even care about beating the market anymore

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:24.839
in the first place.

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:29.160
Buffett has already said that Berkshire is
100% prepared for his death, and I assume

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.450
that this applies to Munger as well given
that he’s 98 years old.

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:36.320
Though both of them are nearing the end of
their lives, there’s no doubt that they’ll

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.250
be remembered as a legendary investing duo
for generations to come.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.320
Would you guys invest in Berkshire Hathaway
today?

